Baralipston, among logicians, a term denoting the first indirect mode of the first figure of syllogism. A syllogism in baralipston, is when the two first propositions are general, and the third particular, the middle term being the subject in the first proposition and the predicate in the second. The following is of this kind:

BA. Every evil ought to be feared;
RA. Every violent passion is an evil;
LIP. Therefore something that ought to be feared is a violent passion.

among Logicians, the firft mode of the firft figure of syllogifms. A syllogifm in barbara is one whereof all the proportions are univerfal and affirmative; the middle term being the fubject of the firft proposition, and attribute in the second.

*Examp.* Bar. Every wicked man is miserable;
Ba. All tyrants are wicked men;
Ra. Therefore all tyrants are miserable.
